Write the image prompt for this story's COVER — the single illustration a reader
sees before they have read a word. Return two fields: prompt (the image
generator's instructions for the cover art) and alt (the alt text for it).

The cover is ATMOSPHERIC and SPOILER-SAFE. It sets the mood and genre a reader is
about to enter — like a back-cover blurb in a picture. Show setting, atmosphere,
genre iconography, and at most one emblematic object or silhouette. Do NOT show
how the story turns out: no plot events, no ending imagery, no character's fate,
no reveal of who did what. When in doubt, stay wide and evocative rather than
specific.

VISION: {{ vision.genre }}{% if vision.subgenre %} ({{ vision.subgenre }}){% endif %},
{{ vision.tone }}. Themes: {{ vision.themes | join("; ") }}.
Premise: {{ vision.premise }}

ART DIRECTION (the cover must look like the rest of the book):
- style: {{ direction.style }}
- palette: {{ direction.palette }}
{% if direction.influences %}- influences: {{ direction.influences | join(", ") }}
{% endif %}{% if direction.notes %}- notes: {{ direction.notes }}
{% endif %}

Write the prompt as a single vivid description an image model can render directly:
the scene or emblem, the light and mood, and a line pinning the style/palette
above. Leave room at the top or bottom of the frame for a title to be set later in
the layout — describe the composition so it does not fill every corner. Name no
specific plot beat; establish the world, not its outcome. The cover is portrait
(2:3); compose for a tall frame.

Then write `alt`: one sentence of {{ alt_min_words }}-{{ alt_max_words }} words telling a reader who
cannot see the cover what is on it — subject, setting, mood, in that order. Start
with the subject; do not write "Cover illustration of", "Image of" or "Picture of",
which a screen reader announces already. It is spoiler-safe on the same terms as
the prompt: describe the picture, never the story's outcome.
